.TH DEFERE 1 "2026-08-22" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
defere \- file the thing you were about to write a paragraph about
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B defere
.I '<one line>'
--project
.I name
.br
.B defere
.I '<one line>'
--human
.I '<why>'
.br
.B defere
.I '<one line>'
--unroutable
.I '<why>'
.br
.B defere
--scan
.br
.B defere
--ledger
.SH DESCRIPTION
Files a deferral as a GitHub issue and accumulates the
.B <!-- DEFERRED -->
block that every pull-request body in this estate must carry. The block is a
receipt; the issue is the queue. Nothing else puts unfinished work where
.BR next-issue (1)
can select it,
.B tempo.sh
can pace it and
.B decision-rot.sh
can notice it was abandoned.
.SH WHY THIS IS A VERB
Until 2026-08-22 this shipped as
.B bin/defere.sh
only, classified LOCAL, carried onto no bashified branch. No self-dev account
holds a realisateur checkout, so no agent in the fleet could run it: the only
door for answering the deferral ledger honestly could not be opened by the
accounts doing the work.
.PP
The cost of that is measurable. The
.B DEFERRED
ledger is answered honestly in 41 of 120 recent merged pull requests (34%).
.BR DELIVERS ,
which has the same grammar, the same enforcement and no actuator at all, is
answered honestly in 2 of 292 (0.7%). A ledger is answered honestly exactly
when honesty is cheaper than the escape hatch, and
.B - none
is always free.
.SH ROUTES
Exactly one, chosen explicitly.
.B --project
files on that repository.
.B --human
records that a person is required and why.
.B --unroutable
records that nothing can own it yet.
.PP
There is no default. An unroutable item silently assigned to a person is
indistinguishable from one that genuinely needs them, and a guessed
destination is how a deferral disappears; a project that does not resolve is
refused with the
.B --unroutable
form printed, never redirected.
.SH SCAN
.B defere --scan
reports what this branch DELETED that a surviving file still names. Each
finding prints the referring file and line and the ready-to-run command that
files it.
.PP
A line whose entire content is the deleted path is a manifest entry, not a
dependency, and is skipped. A line that says the file is gone is a RETRACTION
and is also skipped: this estate propagates mechanism well and retracts claims
badly, so a scan that scolded an author for writing "deleted 2026-08-22" would
tax the one behaviour it wants more of.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
filed, or printed under --dry-run, --ledger or a clean --scan
.TP
.B 1
could not file, or --scan found dangling references
.TP
.B 2
usage error, including refusing to choose a route
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: gh unavailable, or no merge-base to scan against
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gh (1)
--delivers, the same actuator for the DELIVERS ledger.
